Lab 13: Manage End-to-End Secure Services in OpenStack
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Implement mutual TLS (mTLS) for secure service-to-service authentication in OpenStack
Configure Keystone tokens for secure API access
Enforce Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) for permissions management
Verify end-to-end security across OpenStack services
Prerequisites
Basic understanding of OpenStack architecture
Access to an OpenStack deployment (DevStack or production)
Admin privileges on the OpenStack environment
OpenSSL installed on the controller node
Familiarity with Linux command line
Lab Setup
Ensure all OpenStack services are running:
sudo systemctl list-units | grep openstack
Verify Keystone is operational:
openstack token issue
Task 1: Implement Mutual TLS for Service-to-Service Authentication
Subtask 1.1: Generate Certificates
Create a Certificate Authority (CA):
openssl genrsa -out ca.key 4096
openssl req -new -x509 -days 365 -key ca.key -out ca.crt
Generate server certificates for each service (example for Nova):
openssl genrsa -out nova.key 4096
openssl req -new -key nova.key -out nova.csr
openssl x509 -req -days 365 -in nova.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -out nova.crt
Subtask 1.2: Configure Services for mTLS
Edit Nova configuration:
sudo vi /etc/nova/nova.conf
Add these lines under [DEFAULT]:

ssl_cert_file = /etc/nova/ssl/nova.crt
ssl_key_file = /etc/nova/ssl/nova.key
ca_file = /etc/nova/ssl/ca.crt
use_ssl = True
Repeat for other services (Neutron, Cinder, etc.) with their respective certificates.
Subtask 1.3: Verify mTLS Connection
Test Nova API with curl:
curl --cert nova.crt --key nova.key --cacert ca.crt https://<controller-ip>:8774/v2.1
Expected Output: Should return Nova API information without SSL errors.

Task 2: Use Keystone Tokens for Secure API Access
Subtask 2.1: Configure Token Provider
Edit Keystone configuration:
sudo vi /etc/keystone/keystone.conf
Ensure these settings:

[token]
provider = fernet
expiration = 3600
Initialize Fernet keys:
sudo keystone-manage fernet_setup --keystone-user keystone --keystone-group keystone
Subtask 2.2: Test Token Authentication
Get a token:
openstack token issue
Expected Output: Should display token details including expiration time.

Use token to access Nova API:
curl -H "X-Auth-Token: <token>" http://<controller-ip>:8774/v2.1
Task 3: Enforce Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
Subtask 3.1: Create Custom Roles
Create a new role:
openstack role create security_auditor
Assign role to user:
openstack role add --project admin --user admin security_auditor
Subtask 3.2: Configure Policy Files
Edit Nova policy file:
sudo vi /etc/nova/policy.json
Add custom rule:

{
    "context_is_admin": "role:admin or role:security_auditor",
    "compute:get_all": "rule:context_is_admin"
}
Verify policy enforcement:
openstack server list
Expected Outcome: Only users with admin or security_auditor role should see all VMs.

Verification and Testing
Comprehensive security check:
openstack security group list
openstack network list
Verify only authorized users can access these resources.

Check service communication:
sudo tail -f /var/log/nova/nova-api.log | grep SSL
Should show successful TLS handshakes.

Troubleshooting Tips
SSL errors: Verify certificate paths and permissions
Token failures: Check Fernet keys synchronization across nodes
Permission denied: Verify policy.json syntax and role assignments
Service communication issues: Check firewall rules for service ports
Conclusion
In this lab, you implemented end-to-end security for OpenStack services by:

Establishing mutual TLS authentication between services
Configuring secure token-based authentication with Keystone
Enforcing granular access control through RBAC policies
These measures significantly enhance the security posture of your OpenStack deployment by ensuring:

Encrypted service communication
Strong authentication mechanisms
Least-privilege access control
